1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,750
Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array — LeetCode 26

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:04,600
Problem: nums is sorted; remove duplicates in place so each value appears once, and return k.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,850
Example: nums = [1, 1, 2, 2, 3] — the red 1 and 2 are duplicates

4
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:11,900
Result: k = 3, nums = [1, 2, 3, …]; the rest is ignored

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:16,600
Hint: the array is sorted, so duplicates are always adjacent — compare each value to the last unique one

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:21,350
Approach: two pointers — f scans, s marks the last unique; when they differ, it's a new unique

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:25,450
Start: s = 0 (last unique), f scans from index 1

8
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:27,980
f = 1: compare nums[1] with nums[s]

9
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:30,570
1 = 1 → duplicate, skip

10
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:32,560
f = 2: compare nums[2] with nums[s]

11
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:36,800
2 ≠ 1 → new unique: advance s, copy it

12
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,790
f = 3: compare nums[3] with nums[s]

13
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:41,380
2 = 2 → duplicate, skip

14
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:43,370
f = 4: compare nums[4] with nums[s]

15
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:47,610
3 ≠ 2 → new unique: advance s, copy it

16
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:51,450
Loop ends → k = s + 1 = 3

17
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:55,000
The first k = 3 elements are the unique values. Time O(n), space O(1)

18
